   "text": {
    "L1": "Congruent triangles have the same shape and the same size.",
    "L2": "AAA is a myth: equal angles fix the shape, but not the size.",
    "L3": "The four useful tests are SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS.",
    "L4": "SSS uses three equal sides. SAS uses two sides and the angle between them.",
    "L5": "ASA uses two angles and the side between them. RHS uses a right angle, the hypotenuse and one other side.",
    "L6": "In the worked example, the two sides and the included 60-degree angle match, so the triangles are congruent by SAS.",
    "L7": "SSA fails because the angle is not between the sides, and two different triangles can be made.",
    "L8": "Matching vertices must stay in order: if A matches Q, B matches R and C matches P, write ΔABC ≅ ΔQRP.",
    "L9": "Now choose the rule for the worked example."
   },
